{"agent_id":"pp3g-MMlK-adversarial-review-of-the-tools-monorepo-migration","agent_seq":3,"timestamp":"2026-08-14T19:21:17.628587140Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"d9e6075b-9f5c-43d4-9d2f-e9c0bdf48b7a","comment_uuid":"81437531-02b4-46c1-a2cf-a12e6f058064","author":"pp3g-MMlK-adversarial-review-of-the-tools-monorepo-migration","content":"# ADVERSARIAL REVIEW — Tools monorepo migration plan (#354, D1–D7 + 6-step implementation)\n\n## Review Summary: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe plan is directionally sound: the drift inventory is accurate (I independently verified every claim), the reverse-sync direction (D2) is correct, D1's choice of ASES as plugin authority is validated by functional diff, and D6/D7 match on-disk reality. But the plan's central pillar — D4's sync mechanism — has three blocking gaps (non-existent tripn `.ases/` boundary, a D3↔D4 contradiction on rules, and no enforcement point for the canonical-source claim), and step 5 removes live runtime hooks without a re-deploy step. These must be resolved before execution.\n\n### MUST FIX (blocking)\n\n1. **D4's tripn leg references a boundary that does not exist.** Verified: `/home/claude-code/projects/tripn-astro/.ases` → `No such file or directory`; a recursive `*ases*` find in tripn-astro returns 0 matches. The supporting doc cited by #353 (`EDASES-methodology-enforcement.md`) is absent from ASES, Tools, crosslink, and rock-solid-sites. D4 says sync-tooling.sh pushes to tripn \"via its .ases/ boundary\" — as written, step 3 cannot be built. Fix: define the concrete tripn path (tripn is already a crosslink-tracked repo with `.opencode/` and `.crosslink/`; sync would write `tripn/.opencode/plugins` + `tripn/.crosslink/rules` directly), or create+document the `.ases/` boundary first, or drop the tripn leg from this migration's scope.\n\n2. **D3 and D4 contradict on rules — sync-tooling.sh will clobber layer overrides.** D3 lists `.claude/skills + .crosslink/rules` as MIRRORS \"(documented, not synced): binary + ASES overrides\". D4 says the script copies \"(plugins, wrappers, skills, rules) into each layer repo (.opencode/plugins, .claude/skills, .crosslink/rules)\". If it copies `Tools/.crosslink/rules/*` into `ASES/.crosslink/rules/*`, it wipes ASES overrides that exist TODAY: ASES global.md has a \"Background processes\" rule (nohup discipline) that Tools' copy lacks (verified by diff), and per #354 ASES adds kickoff.md. The plan has no file-level ownership map for rules. Fix: enumerate which rules files are warehouse-owned vs layer-owned (e.g., only ship the binary-distributed subset, never global.md/knowledge.md/project.md/kickoff.md), or exclude `.crosslink/rules` from sync entirely (D3 already says mirror-not-synced).\n\n3. **No enforcement point for the canonical-source claim — drift will persist by design.** The #274 pattern D4 cites is a consumer-side fail-loud runtime guard (the live opencode wrapper checks the resolved binary on every invocation). D4's version/checksum marker has no defined consumer-side check: nothing specifies who runs it, when, or what fails loudly. Worse, D1 inverts the development workflow: today the guard plugin is developed in ASES (where `.opencode/opencode.json` loads it) and would be pushed to Tools; after the migration the canonical copy lives in Tools but every consumer still loads from its own `.opencode/plugins`. The next plugin fix will naturally land in ASES again, and Tools/plugins silently goes stale — the same drift, relocated. Fix: define a consumption-time check (e.g., guard-plugin startup marker check, or a preflight/rule injection that compares checksums and fails loudly on mismatch) before declaring Tools canonical — otherwise \"canonical\" is aspirational.\n\n4. **Step 5 (rm gitignored `.claude/hooks+commands+mcp` from Tools fs) has no re-deploy step and silently changes Tools repo runtime behavior.** These are LIVE artifacts: `Tools/.claude/hooks/work-check.py`, `prompt-guard.py`, `heartbeat.py`, `session-start.py` are wired via `Tools/.claude/settings.json` and loaded every session. #354's own evidence found the on-disk hooks match an OLDER embedded version (returncode==2 logic) than the fork's current embedded resources (returncode!=2). So: (a) rm without re-init → Tools repo agents run with NO hooks (issue-tracking gate, prompt-guard, heartbeat all gone); (b) rm + re-init from the current binary → hook BEHAVIOR changes (returncode semantics) without a fork rebuild. The plan states neither the re-init step nor the behavior change. Fix: specify the exact sequence (rm → `crosslink init` from a known binary version → verify hook behavior), or defer step 5 until the binary is rebuilt to match.\n\n### SHOULD CONSIDER (non-blocking)\n\n- **D2's merge premise is stale; and there is no deploy path back to `~/.local/bin`.** Verified: live `~/.local/bin/claude` already CONTAINS the memory-scope block (MEMORY_SCOPE + systemd-run) AND `--agent`/CROSSLINK_AGENT_TYPE/`--auto` mapping. Tools/scripts/claude has memory-scope but lacks `--agent`, still passes `--dangerously-skip-permissions` through (live maps to `--auto`), and has a prompt-quoting bug (`${PROMPT:+\"\\\"\\\"\"}` emits literal quotes). So step 1 is effectively \"replace Tools copy with live copy\" — low risk, but say so and add a post-merge functional check (arg mapping, quoting). Bigger gap: after reverse-sync, Tools/scripts is canonical but `~/.local/bin` remains the live deployed copy with no deploy step and no drift check — the two copies will silently diverge again. (TOOLING.md §1.1/§1.2 already falsely claims the copies are \"identical\".)\n- **D1's \"newest = authoritative\" criterion is weak; the functional diff is the evidence — record it.** Verified: ASES crosslink-guard.ts is a functional superset of tripn's (session-keyed chat.params/chat.message agent capture; no tripn-only features lost) and is repo-agnostic (0 hardcoded ASES/tripn/projects/ strings — portability confirmed). Same for orchestrator-guard.ts. rtk-guard.ts is same size everywhere (13.6K; not byte-diffed). Note: tripn's hook-config.json by_type.orchestrator gates \"git worktree remove\" and its comment_discipline=required, signing_enforcement=enforced differ from ASES — these are config-side and survive a plugin push, but see next point.\n- **hook-config.json drift is out of scope yet is half the enforcement surface.** The plugin's effective behavior = plugin version × per-repo `.crosslink/hook-config.json`. Verified: ASES and tripn configs differ materially (by_type builder entry only in ASES; comment_discipline encouraged vs required; signing_enforcement audit vs enforced; agent.type absent in tripn). The plan makes plugins single-source but leaves configs to drift silently. At minimum, TOOLING.md's source-of-truth map should explicitly list hook-config.json as layer-owned and note the residual drift risk.\n- **Sync target matrix is undefined.** \"Each layer repo\" — today only ASES exists on disk (no /home/claude-code/projects/EDASES or Execution dirs; verified via `ls ~/projects`). sync-tooling.sh must tolerate absent targets. Also \"wrappers\" in D4's copy list has no destination — layer repos don't consume wrappers; only `~/.local/bin` does, and the plan never says the script deploys there.\n- **Knowledge-page step dropped.** #354's analysis step 6 (migrate the catalog knowledge page) is absent from the 6-step plan. The `tooling` knowledge page exists as a thin pointer to Tools/TOOLING.md, but its \"last audited\" date (2026-07-09) and prose will go stale; step 4 should refresh it (cheap).\n- **D6's \"cataloged in TOOLING.md §16\" is true but thin.** §16 exists and points at /home/claude-code/projects/crosslink, but never uses the word \"fork\", doesn't state it's a fork with an external upstream, and doesn't document it as the canonical source of embedded resources (hooks/skills/rules deployed by crosslink init). Step 4 should strengthen §16 to prevent future agents treating the fork as a Tools-owned artifact.\n\n### NITS\n\n- D1's size figures are stale: issue says 42.1K/37.6K/33.5K; measured 41.2K/36.7K/32.7K (same ordering).\n- \"71 agent-related refs vs 32\" is a weak metric — the functional diff (superset) is the actual evidence; record that instead.\n- Steps 1/2 say \"Commit on Tools repo\" without preconditions: Tools has uncommitted changes (scripts/claude, .crosslink/rules/global.md) and its branch state is unverified — commit/stash first, verify branch, then commit.\n- Cross-repo handoff: implementation will be tracked on the Tools repo's own crosslink tracker while the plan/decisions live on the ASES tracker (#354). Step 6's created Tools issues should explicitly reference #354/D1–D7 to avoid orphaned implementation issues.\n\n### NOTABLE POSITIVES\n\n- The drift inventory is accurate — I independently verified every claim: 3-way plugin divergence, live wrappers ahead of Tools/scripts (#274 guard, --agent, CROSSLINK_AGENT_TYPE), tripn-astro bundle stale, hooks/commands/mcp gitignored (.gitignore confirms), TOOLING.md catalog drift (§1.1/§1.2 claim identical copies that are not).\n- D1's authoritative-version choice is validated: ASES plugin is a functional superset AND repo-agnostic (no hardcoded paths), so pushing it to Tools and later to tripn is portable.\n- D6 (single Tools repo + fork sibling) matches verified reality: the fork is a separate Rust repo with its own remotes; Tools/crosslink/ contains only moe-config.env.\n- D5 (keep stale archive + STALE marker) is right; the bundle is genuinely stale and TOOLING.md §3.6 currently has no STALE marker (plan step 4 adds it).\n- D7 (repo-of-repos, no submodules, decide-now-build-later) is sound and matches the current \"logically grouped\" state.\n\n### WHAT-NOT-TESTED (my negative space)\n\n- Did NOT execute sync-tooling.sh (doesn't exist) or test any marker enforcement behavior.\n- Did NOT run `crosslink init` or rebuild the fork binary — the hooks-stale-version claim rests on #354's evidence; I did not byte-diff the binary's embedded resources.\n- Did NOT byte-diff rtk-guard.ts (sizes identical across repos; full diff not run).\n- Did NOT inspect Tools' git branch/history (sandbox blocked `git -C`); the uncommitted-changes claim is corroborated by file reads and #354, not by git status in Tools.\n- Did NOT execute the ASES plugin against tripn's hook-config.json (config compatibility reasoned, not run).\n- Did NOT read reviewer #358 (luna) / #360 (nemotron) verdicts (independence mandate).\n\n## VERDICT\n\nCHANGES REQUESTED — 4 blocking findings. The plan's architecture (Tools as warehouse source + sync distribution + fork stays sibling) is right, but D4 must be re-specified (tripn boundary, rules ownership, enforcement point) and step 5 re-sequenced before implementation. None of the decisions D1–D7 need reversal; they need the sync mechanism made concrete and safe.","created_at":"2026-08-14T19:21:17.621093456Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:TbqAvGerjOwEcLKkPxec2/2AsovdzlvEEnC8pWhmLo4","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgqsg/Gn8dfAb8BYU4ASgNeoFz2vpjh3MhGCnIoDuudbgAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAxsXZnomxmP841fyxwZgWdfZJbfwGjmnI9FgZLFELUfsI16PAG5r+H7nX6V1XoOF6TJCEbMgY3CqIgqb/e+30Cg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:TbqAvGerjOwEcLKkPxec2/2AsovdzlvEEnC8pWhmLo4","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgqsg/Gn8dfAb8BYU4ASgNeoFz2vpjh3MhGCnIoDuudbgAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQACsqcT3Wcm9r+nFvmsNIZPRkr2Ovbat+msmIHH/DkrqQnw81R9v3DIZrRcR7ScopRwvEQE1NfUYAwR5KfWvyQQ="}
